</p>
<p>To make the =CE=94 <em>look</em> bigger than the circle while keeping th=
e same bounding size, pros use optical weight tricks, not scaling. Here are=
 the techniques that achieve your goal:</p>
<p>**1. Stroke Weight Compensation **<br>
Right now the circle and =CE=94 have the same thin stroke. Because a triang=
le has less ink than a circle, it looks smaller. If we make the =CE=94 stro=
ke 15-20% thicker than the circle stroke, same outer size, the =CE=94 will =
instantly look bolder and visually larger than the circle. Nothing scales.[=
Recommended]</p>
<p><span class=3D"font-semibold" data-streamdown=3D"strong">2. Optical Over=
shoot</span><br>
In luxury wordmarks, sharp points must extend 3-5% beyond a round shape to =
<em>appear</em> equal. We would keep the circle size identical and let just=
 the 3 points of the =CE=94 push 2-3px <em>past</em> the inner edge of the =
circle. It still measures the same, but your eye reads it as bigger and per=
fectly balanced.</p>
<p><span class=3D"font-semibold" data-streamdown=3D"strong">3. Filled vs Ou=
tlined Contrast</span><br>
Keep both at the exact same size, but make the =CE=94 solid filled gold and=
